  First question — and it changes the shape of everything downstream:

  [1mThe fork:[0m how much of "validation" becomes reusable. Right now [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m does rule-checking, and the submit
  listener separately does field-reading and error-reporting ([38;5;153mconsole.error[39m). A shared module can take one, two, or all
  three of those.

  - [1mRules only[0m — a library of predicates ([38;5;153mrequired[39m, [38;5;153mminLength[39m, [38;5;153memail[39m) plus a [38;5;153mvalidate(values, schema)[39m. Cheapest, no DOM
    opinions, each form keeps its own wiring. Costs: every new form re-writes the same read-fields-and-show-errors
    boilerplate.
  - [1mRules + field reading[0m — the above, plus something that pulls values out of a [38;5;153m<form>[39m element for you. Middle ground;
    ties the module to the DOM but not to any error-display style.
  - [1mRules + wiring + error display[0m — a [38;5;153mattachValidation(formEl, schema)[39m that owns submit interception, validation, and
    rendering messages into the page. Most reuse per new form, but it fixes an error-display convention now, and that's
    the expensive one to change later since it touches markup in every form.

  Cheap to change later: adding rules, renaming them. Expensive: the error-display contract and whether the module
  touches the DOM at all.

  [1mMy recommendation: rules + field reading.[0m The real repetition across forms is the rule-checking and the
  get-values-from-inputs loop; error display tends to differ per form (inline vs. banner vs. toast), and the current
  code doesn't display errors at all — it logs. Committing to a display convention before you have a second form is the
  guess most likely to be wrong.
